<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Galuh Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">669 CE – 1482 CE (approx.) · capital Kawali</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sundanese kingdom in the eastern Priangan of West Java, the twin and rival of the Sunda Kingdom until their union in 1482.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to Sundanese tradition, chiefly the 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carita-parahyangan">Carita Parahyangan</a>, Galuh emerged around 669 when the lands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tarumanagara">Tarumanagara</a> divided along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citarum-river">Citarum River</a>, Galuh taking the east and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom</a> the west. Its legendary founder Wretikandayun is said to have ruled from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciamis">Ciamis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Galuh was an inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing realm with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and indigenous Sundanese religious traditions. Its best-attested period is the 14th and 15th centuries, when kings ruled from Kawali. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana-gede">Astana Gede</a> inscriptions there record <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niskala-wastu-kancana">Prabu Raja Wastu</a>, identified with Niskala Wastu Kancana, fortifying the town and urging his successors to rule justly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1482 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-baduga-maharaja">Jayadewata</a>, known as Sri Baduga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>, inherited both thrones and moved the united court to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Pakuan Pajajaran</a>, merging Galuh into the Sunda Kingdom. Galuh later came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram</a>, and its name survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciamis">Ciamis</a> regional identity.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/galuh-kingdom.jpg" alt="Galuh Kingdom" width="320" height="163"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunda_Kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>669 CE – 1482 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kawali</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wretikandayun, Niskala Wastu Kancana</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism and Sundanese Wiwitan</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Sundanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Indonesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Galuh</dd></dl></aside></div>
